4. PAGE 2: ENERGY BANDS (#bands)
=====================================================

Layout: main column and sidebar.

Main column:
a) Material buttons: "Conductor", "Semiconductor", "Insulator", "n-type". Selected one is highlighted.
b) Sub-selector (visible only for Semiconductor): "Ge · 0.67 eV", "Si · 1.10 eV", "GaAs · 1.43 eV" (small buttons). Default Si.
c) Band canvas card (flush): header with the title (label style, showing "Silicon (Si)", "Conductor · Cu / Al", "Insulator · diamond / quartz", or "n-type semiconductor") and a pill "E_g = 1.10 eV" (conductor: "E_g = 0 eV (overlap)"). Canvas height 440px, clickable (click on the valence band excites an electron). Legend: "Valence electron (bound)" (blue dot), "Conduction electron (free)" (amber dot), "Hole" (dashed red ring), "Donor level" (violet dot).
d) A two-column pair of cards (stacks to one column under 640px):
   - Temperature card: "Temperature T" with value "300 K · 27 °C" (updates to "x K · y °C"); slider 0..800 step 10, default 300; scale labels "0 K", "300 K", "800 K".
   - Photon card: "Photon energy hν" with value "1.2 eV"; slider 0.2..6 step 0.1, default 1.2; a "Pulse" small button beside it; note "Excites an electron when hν ≥ E_g."

Sidebar:
e) Electrical conduction card: label "Electrical conduction" and a switch "Apply voltage" (default off). Stats: "Current I" (value plus unit "mA"; conductor unit "A") and "Free e⁻ / holes" (value "free / holes"). Notes below (muted): one explanation line and a type line (both updated per frame from state). Toast area for photon messages.
f) Explanation card (prose): material-specific text (see 4.5).
g) Key principles card (prose): label "Key principles", then three paragraphs, each with a bold lead-in:
   - "Discrete levels." Electrons occupy permitted energy states only, never inside the forbidden gap.
   - "Higher energy, farther out." Outer valence electrons have the most potential energy. Once freed into the conduction band, they sit at the highest energy.
   - "Conduction needs room." Current flows only when mobile charges sit in states next to empty, available levels.

4.1 LAYOUT (band canvas, height 440):
- bandX = 44; bandW = min(300, w·0.42); cbTop = 40; cbH = 80; cbBot = cbTop + cbH.
- Gap height: conductor → −10 (overlap); insulator → 150; else min(130, max(40, Eg·75)).
- vbTop = cbBot + gap; vbH = 90; vbBot = vbTop + vbH; donorY = cbBot + 18.
- Eg: Ge 0.67, Si 1.10, GaAs 1.43, n-type 1.10 (donor band just under the CB), insulator 5.5, conductor 0.

4.2 BAND DRAWING:
- Energy axis at x = bandX − 18, from y = 35 to vbBot + 25, gray line with an arrowhead on top. Labels "Energy" (top), "higher" (near top), "lower" (near bottom), right-aligned at x − 6.
- Conduction band rectangle (sky-tinted 12% gradient fading out downward, sky outline, title "Conduction band" at top-left, subtitle "Electrons free to carry current" below the title).
- Valence band rectangle (blue-tinted 10% gradient, blue outline, title "Valence band", subtitle "Electrons bound to atoms").
- Forbidden gap (not conductor): gray-tinted fill between cbBot and vbTop, a violet measurement bar on the right side (with small caps), and the centered label "Forbidden gap · E_g = x.xx eV" (violet, 11px semi-bold), plus "No electron states exist here" muted 10px below if the gap is taller than 40 px.
- Conductor: a dashed amber rectangle straddling cbBot and the label "Bands overlap · no forbidden gap" in amber, centered at cbBot + 4.
- n-type: a dashed violet donor line across the band at donorY with the right-aligned label "Donor level · E_d ≈ 0.05 eV below CB" just above the line.
- Atomic lattice panel (only drawn if the space to the right of the band is ≥ 120 px): a rounded white/surface box at x = bandX + bandW + 40, from cbTop to vbBot + 10 tall, with the title "Atomic lattice". Grid of atoms: columns = clamp(floor(width/90), 2..3), rows 2, positions evenly spaced. Each atom: dashed blue orbit ring r 22 at 22% alpha, nucleus dot r 7 (gray, or violet for n-type). Caption at the bottom, centered: "Applied field: electrons drift (− → +)" (sky, when the voltage is on) or "Thermal motion only, no net drift" (muted).

4.3 PARTICLES AND THERMAL PHYSICS:
- Valence electrons: 32, random positions in the valence band, random slow velocities. Each has state 'v' (valence), 'c' (conduction), a target y ty (for smooth transitions, y moves 12% of the distance per frame), and a laser flag.
- Conductor: the first 18 electrons start in the conduction band. Thermal update marks the first 20 as conduction.
- n-type: 8 donor sites at donorY, evenly spaced across the band (x = bandX + 25 + i·spacing). Ionized donors (count = ceil(T/180 · 8) if T > 50, else 0) move in the conduction band with thermal jitter. Non-ionized stay at their site.
- Semiconductor thermal excitation: maxEx = round(exp(−Eg / (kT·15)) · 16), with kT = 8.617e−5 · T, applied only when T > 40 K. If there are fewer conduction electrons than maxEx, promote valence electrons to the conduction band (targets in the conduction band) and add a hole for each. If there are more, demote the surplus back to the valence band and remove holes.
- Insulator: all non-laser conduction electrons return to the valence band; holes are cleared.
- Movement: valence electrons move with thermal velocity vib = min(2.5, T/300 · 0.9), bounce inside the valence band. Conduction electrons move with the same jitter plus drift (1.8 px/frame when the voltage is on), wrap at the right edge, bounce in the conduction band.
- Holes: drift with jitter inside the valence band.
- Drawing: valence electrons blue r 4 with white "−"; conduction electrons amber r 4.5 with dark "−"; holes dashed red rings r 4.5 with a "+"; donor electrons violet (or amber when ionized) r 4.

4.4 PHOTON AND EXCITATION:
- A photon packet: wavy yellow-gold line (#ca8a04) with a label "hν = x eV", launched from (x = 10, y = midway between cbBot and vbTop) and moving toward (band center x, vbTop + 25). It is absorbed when it arrives (distance < 15 px) or after its progress reaches 1.
- On absorption: if hν ≥ E_g, a random valence electron is promoted to the conduction band with a hole left behind (laser flag set, so it is not reset by thermal updates) and the toast says "Absorbed: hν = X eV ≥ E_g = Y eV, electron promoted." Revert after 5 seconds (unless the material is a conductor): it falls back to the valence band and its hole is removed. Otherwise the toast says "Transmitted: hν = X eV is below E_g = Y eV."
- "Pulse" button launches one packet at the current photon energy. A click on the valence band area (within 10 px vertically) excites one electron directly (toast "Electron excited by direct click.").

4.5 MATERIAL TEXT (explanation card):
- Conductor: "Conductor. The conduction and valence bands overlap, so there is no forbidden gap. Thermal motion alone places many electrons in the conduction band, giving high conductivity at ordinary temperatures."
- Semiconductor: "[Ge / Silicon (Si) / Gallium arsenide (GaAs)] has a band gap of [0.67 / 1.10 / 1.43] eV. At 0 K every electron sits in the valence band and the material is an insulator. Heat, or light with hν ≥ E_g, lifts electrons into the conduction band and leaves holes behind."
- Insulator: "Insulator. The gap exceeds 5 eV, far more than thermal energy can bridge. Electrons stay bound unless an extreme photon or field frees them, and the resulting breakdown is usually permanent."
- n-type: "n-type semiconductor. Pentavalent donor atoms add energy levels about 0.05 eV below the conduction band. Room temperature is enough to ionize nearly every donor, flooding the conduction band with free electrons."

4.6 METERS (updated every frame):
- Free carriers = conduction electrons + conduction donors. Holes = number of holes.
- Readout "free / holes" in the stat.
- Voltage off: current "0.00", note "Voltage off: electrons move randomly, with no net drift.", type "No net conduction".
- Voltage on and free = 0: current "0.00", note "No free carriers in the conduction band.", type "Blocked · insulating state".
- Voltage on and free > 0: current = (free × 1.42) with two decimals; note "N free carriers drifting under the field."; type "Low-resistance conductor" (conductor) or "Active conduction" (others). Unit shows "A" for conductors and "mA" otherwise.
- Toggling materials or sub-materials resets the particles and updates the title, pill, and explanation.

